# Add Dockerfile and Kubernetes Deployment for intern-task-app

## Summary

Added Docker and Kubernetes configuration to containerize and deploy the `intern-task-app` Python HTTP service.

## Changes Made

- Added a Dockerfile using the lightweight `python:3.12-slim` base image.
- Configured the application to listen on port 8080 using the `PORT` environment variable.
- Configured the container to run as a non-root user.
- Added a Kubernetes Deployment named `intern-task-app`.
- Added readiness and liveness HTTP probes targeting `/healthz`.
- Configured CPU and memory requests and limits.
- Added a ClusterIP Service exposing port 80 and forwarding traffic to container port 8080.
- Set `imagePullPolicy: Never` because the image is built locally and loaded into the kind cluster.

## Verification

Commands used:

```bash
docker build -t intern-task-app:local .

docker run --rm -d \
  --name intern-task-app-test \
  -p 8080:8080 \
  -e PORT=8080 \
  intern-task-app:local

curl -i http://localhost:8080/healthz
curl -i http://localhost:8080/

docker stop intern-task-app-test

kind create cluster --name intern-task

kind load docker-image intern-task-app:local --name intern-task

kubectl apply -f k8s/

kubectl rollout status deployment/intern-task-app --timeout=60s

kubectl port-forward svc/intern-task-app 8080:80
```

Also tested the `/healthz` and `/` endpoints through the Kubernetes Service.

